# Copy to .env and fill in. No real secrets in git (ADR-0025).
# Dedicated least-privilege role + database (not the postgres superuser).
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://fsm:<password>@localhost:5432/fsm?schema=public"
# Required. Boot aborts (#98) if unset, left as the old "dev-access-secret-change-me" default, or
# shorter than 32 chars. Generate one with: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))"
JWT_ACCESS_SECRET="change-me-to-a-random-32+char-secret"

# ── Test database (isolated) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The vitest e2e suite runs against a SIBLING database, never DATABASE_URL directly: once a real
# AutoPlant sync has loaded the production org graph (~17.9k devices) into your dev DB, full-fleet
# operations (DeviceStateService.recompute, fleet-uptime aggregation) iterate every row and exceed
# the per-test timeout. By default the suite derives the test URL from DATABASE_URL by suffixing the
# db name with `_test` (…/fsm → …/fsm_test); set TEST_DATABASE_URL to override. test/global-setup.ts
# migrates + seeds it automatically before the run — but the database itself is a ONE-TIME superuser
# bootstrap (it needs the PostGIS extension, which the app role cannot create). As a Postgres
# superuser, once per machine:
#   CREATE DATABASE fsm_test OWNER fsm;
#   \c fsm_test
#   CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS postgis;
#   GRANT ALL ON SCHEMA public TO fsm;
# TEST_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://fsm:<password>@localhost:5432/fsm_test?schema=public"
#
# Every vitest run truncates + reseeds this database automatically (test/global-setup.ts, #180 R2) so
# no orphaned row from a crashed prior run survives into the next one. To reset it by hand (e.g. to
# inspect a clean baseline, or after a manual `psql` edit) without running the suite, build once, then:
#   pnpm build && pnpm test:reset

# ── AutoPlant upstream — read-only MySQL Snapshot source (Issue 04), reachable over VPN ──────────
# Least-privilege READ-ONLY account. Leave UNSET in dev / test / CI: the app boots without it and the
# SnapshotIngestionWorker keeps using its in-memory reader until the real SourceReader is wired.
# Set these in your local .env (never commit real secrets — ADR-0025) and connect to the VPN, then
# verify with: npm run build && npm run autoplant:ping
# The production source spans TWO databases on one host: ap_widgets (live telemetry — tb_vehiclemaster)
# and ap_masters (mst_* masters). BOTH must be set or the app stays on the in-memory reader.
# AUTOPLANT_MYSQL_HOST="10.0.0.25"         # private host — only resolvable/reachable on the VPN
# AUTOPLANT_MYSQL_PORT="3306"
# AUTOPLANT_MYSQL_USER="fsm_readonly"
# AUTOPLANT_MYSQL_PASSWORD="<password>"
# AUTOPLANT_MYSQL_DB_WIDGETS="ap_widgets"  # live telemetry schema (connection default)
# AUTOPLANT_MYSQL_DB_MASTERS="ap_masters"  # master-data schema (queried schema-qualified)
# AUTOPLANT_MYSQL_SSL="false"              # "true" if the server requires TLS
# (legacy AUTOPLANT_MYSQL_DATABASE still works as a fallback for AUTOPLANT_MYSQL_DB_WIDGETS)

# ── Ingestion scheduler (Issue 97 Slice 7) — in-process @nestjs/schedule cron ────────────────────
# OFF by default; enabling is a deliberate ops step AFTER zones are ratified (review B8). Dormant
# whenever AutoPlant is unconfigured (above), so dev/test/CI never need the VPN. Overlapping ticks
# degrade to a logged RUN_IN_PROGRESS skip — the in-flight guards stay the serialization mechanism.
# INGESTION_SCHEDULER_ENABLED="true"       # anything but the literal "true" stays OFF
# INGESTION_MASTERS_CRON="0 2 * * *"       # org-graph refresh — daily, off-hours
# INGESTION_TELEMETRY_CRON="*/30 * * * *"  # snapshot ingest + device-state recompute
# INGESTION_STALE_RUN_MIN="30"             # reap RUNNING runs SILENT this long (Slice 2, review A2).
#                                          # #261: judged on `heartbeat_at`, not `started_at` — a
#                                          # legitimately long master sync is no longer reaped for
#                                          # being slow. Higher than the dispatch threshold on
#                                          # purpose: this one waits on a remote system.
# INGESTION_RECON_MAX_DRIFT="0"            # rows of source-vs-FSM drift tolerated in /health (Slice 5)
# INGESTION_LIFECYCLE_QUIET_RUNS="3"       # consecutive syncs moving no device before /health warns (#218a).
#                                          # Patience, NOT tolerance: lifecycle `drift` has no acceptable
#                                          # non-zero value, so there is deliberately no knob for it.

# ── Auto-recovery pre-check (#229) — bounded by default, no master switch of its own ─────────────
# The pre-check runs INSIDE the telemetry tick (device-state recompute → auto-recovery → ticket
# creation, per fsm-backend-low-level-design.md:615), so INGESTION_SCHEDULER_ENABLED above is its
# only on/off gate — deliberately, because that is the switch the operator already reasons about.
#
# The cap is NOT optional-by-omission: unset means 200, not unlimited. The qualifying backlog is
# ~9,888 tickets whose ping evidence is already on disk, so a first pass with no bound would close
# all of them the moment telemetry is enabled — an operational event arriving as a deploy
# side-effect. Capped, the drain is a sequence of resumable passes (oldest failure cycle first) that
# an operator watches and can stop by changing this one value. In steady state it never binds.
# AUTO_RECOVERY_MAX_PER_PASS="200"         # positive integer, or "unlimited" to remove the bound
#
# Preview what it would close, writing nothing:  npm run autorecovery:dryrun -- --export plan.csv

# ── Business-sweep scheduler (Issue 108) / partition maintenance — the other two master switches ──
# BUSINESS_SWEEPS_ENABLED gates the periodic verification/intraday/cross-zone/report-cube sweeps
# (BusinessSweepSchedulerService); PARTITION_MAINTENANCE_ENABLED gates daily raw_device_snapshots
# partition creation. Both OFF by default, same "anything but the literal 'true' stays OFF" rule.
# BUSINESS_SWEEPS_ENABLED="true"
# PARTITION_MAINTENANCE_ENABLED="true"
#
# ── Dispatch-run liveness and patience (#261 / #260) — two numbers that constrain each other ─────
# DISPATCH_STALE_RUN_MIN is how long a dispatch run may go SILENT (no heartbeat) before the reaper
# presumes its process died, marks it ABORTED and frees its zone claims. DISPATCH_RETRY_DEADLINE_MS
# is how long the 05:00 cron keeps re-asking for a zone another run is holding.
#
#   THE INVARIANT: reap <= retry deadline.
#
# If a crashed holder could outlive the cron's patience, the morning run would spend its entire
# retry window waiting behind a claim nobody is holding and then give up — the exact starvation the
# reaper exists to prevent. 10 min against 15 leaves a full reap cycle of slack. Both defaults are
# stated together in `src/scheduling/dispatch-cron.ts`; change one, re-check the other.
#
# The reap threshold is well above any real run: a run beats at admission and after every zone, so a
# live run is silent only for as long as its slowest SINGLE zone takes, not its whole length.
# DISPATCH_STALE_RUN_MIN="10"              # #261 — reap a run silent this long (heartbeat, not age)
# DISPATCH_RETRY_INTERVAL_MS="60000"       # #260 — how often the patient CRON re-asks for a zone
# DISPATCH_RETRY_DEADLINE_MS="900000"      # #260 — how long it stays patient. **0 = try-once**, the
#                                          # documented rollback: a MANUAL run is never patient
#                                          # regardless, because an operator wants an answer, not a
#                                          # queue.
#
# #182 — the e2e suite NEVER reads these (or any AUTOPLANT_MYSQL_*/INGESTION_*/PARTITION_*/
# DISPATCH_*/BUSINESS_SWEEP* var) from this file. `test/setup-env.ts` is an ALLOWLIST: it deletes every var in
# the application's own namespace and re-sets a fixed test value for the handful the suite needs — so
# a NEW switch added here is neutralised automatically, by default, with no test-harness edit
# required. A spec that genuinely needs one ON passes it through the service's own config-override
# constructor param, never the environment (see #182 R5).

# ── Dev login seed (#194) — the only committed way to get a login on a development database ──────
# Nothing else in committed code writes a `user_credentials` row: `npm run seed` seeds reference data
# only, `prisma/seed-mock-engineers.ts` creates users with no credentials, and the auth fixture is
# called only by the test harness against fsm_test. Without the step below a clean clone boots fine
# and every login 401s, with no diagnostic (login cannot distinguish "no credential row" from "wrong
# password" — deliberately, so it is not an account-enumeration oracle).
#
# Full sequence from a clean clone:
#   npx prisma migrate deploy     # schema
#   npm run build
#   npm run seed                  # zones, plants, tiers, SLA/priority config — reference data only
#   npm run seed:dev              # the 8 logins below                        ← requires the flag
#   npm start
# Order matters: `seed` must precede `seed:dev`, because ZM accounts are scoped by ZONE NAME and
# seeding them against an unseeded database would silently create managers with a null zone_id — a
# login that authenticates and then 403s on every zone-scoped route. `seed:dev` refuses if the
# operational zones are absent rather than doing that.
#
# Accounts created (password below), all idempotent — re-running never rotates an existing password:
#   ops.head@fsm.test  OPERATIONS_HEAD          csm@fsm.test  CENTRAL_SERVICE_MANAGER
#   wm@fsm.test        WAREHOUSE_MANAGER        se.north@fsm.test  SERVICE_ENGINEER (North)
#   zm.north / zm.south / zm.east / zm.west @fsm.test   ZONAL_MANAGER, zones 1-4
# These are the same accounts apps/admin/visual/manifest.mjs CREDS logs in as, so `visual:capture`
# works on a clean machine once this has run.
#
# OFF by default, in every environment. It mints manager-role accounts with a well-known password, so
# it is opted into per machine — set it only once you have confirmed DATABASE_URL points at your
# development database. It is additionally refused outright when NODE_ENV=production, which this flag
# cannot override. Real accounts are provisioned through POST /api/org/users, not this.
# ALLOW_DEV_SEED="true"
#
# Replaces the well-known `correct-password` for the accounts above. Leave unset unless you need to:
# the default is what apps/admin/visual/manifest.mjs and the e2e suite expect, and overriding it here
# means `visual:capture` needs its CREDS changed to match. Blank is rejected rather than falling back
# to the default, so a mistyped value fails loudly instead of quietly seeding the well-known one.
# Only affects accounts whose credential row does not yet exist — it never rotates an existing hash.
# DEV_SEED_PASSWORD="something-else"

# ── Operations Data Explorer (#217) — read-only investigation surface ────────────────────────────
# OH-only, and additionally gated by OPS_EXPLORER_ENABLED. OFF by default in EVERY environment: this
# is a diagnostic instrument, not a product feature, so it is opted into per deployment. When off,
# every /api/ops-explorer/* route returns 404 (not 403 — a disabled feature should not confirm its
# own existence) and the admin nav entry does not render.
# OPS_EXPLORER_ENABLED="true"
#
# The lineage/diagnostics layer on top: source columns, the SQL that ran, bound parameters, query
# timings, raw responses. Defaults ON outside production and OFF when NODE_ENV=production; set it
# explicitly to override in either direction. It is stripped SERVER-SIDE — with it off the deep
# fields are absent from the JSON, so the browser never receives them. Implies OPS_EXPLORER_ENABLED:
# developer mode can never be on while the feature itself is off.
# OPS_EXPLORER_DEVELOPER_MODE="true"

# ── Commissioning view (#232) — the time-to-first-report epoch ───────────────────────────────────
# The instant `device_states.first_reported_at` began being written (migration
# 20260809120000_device_commissioning). The column is filled write-once, so for a device that was
# ALREADY reporting when it landed, that first COALESCE captured a LAST-seen value wearing a
# first-seen name: 15,345 of 23,086 populated values are stamped on the single day the column
# shipped, and 2,138 carry a stamp EARLIER than their own installed_at. Fitments before this instant
# still count as installs and as online, but never contribute a TIMING sample — median TTFR over that
# population is ~8,707 h (≈363 days) against 17.26 h for fitments observed after it.
#
# Configurable because a re-baseline (a backfill, a fresh mirror) moves it, and that must be an
# operator change rather than a code change. Unset means the built-in epoch; an unparseable value
# falls back to it rather than poisoning every timing sample with `Invalid Date`.
# COMMISSIONING_TTFR_EPOCH="2026-08-09T00:00:00.000Z"
